## Claim
Text: "Joe Biden said he wants to ban fracking in the US."
Claim author: Charlie Kirk of the conservative student organization Turning Point USA
Claim date: October 23, 2020
Claim origin: https://web.archive.org/web/20210506043822/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hV8_Bunawqg

## Initial Q&A
### Did Joe Biden state during his 2020 presidential campaign that he wanted to completely ban hydraulic fracturing (fracking) in the United States?
Answer: No, Joe Biden did not state that he wanted to completely ban fracking in the United States; in fact, he explicitly denied this during his 2020 campaign. At an event in Pittsburgh, Biden stated, “I am not banning fracking. Let me say that again: I am not banning fracking.” While he promised to block new fracking permits on federal lands, his campaign clarified that he did not support a total ban on the practice, which primarily takes place on private and state-owned land.

Source URL: https://grist.org/politics/why-wont-biden-ban-fracking-speech-pennsylvania/

### What was Joe Biden's official campaign policy platform regarding hydraulic fracturing (fracking) on federal lands during the 2020 United States presidential election?
Answer: During the 2020 presidential election, Joe Biden's policy platform regarding fracking on federal lands was that he "wouldn't allow new fracking leases on federal land," while maintaining that he would not shut down the fracking industry as a whole.

Source URL: https://www.newsweek.com/biden-says-fracking-jobs-wont-chopping-block-if-hes-elected-1516793

### What was Joe Biden's official campaign policy platform regarding hydraulic fracturing (fracking) on private lands during the 2020 United States presidential election?
Answer: During the 2020 presidential election, Joe Biden's campaign platform did not support a ban on fracking on private lands. Biden repeatedly stated that "fracking is not going to be on the chopping block" and clarified that he "wouldn't shut down the fracking industry but merely wouldn't allow new fracking leases on federal land," leaving fracking on private lands permitted.

Source URL: https://www.newsweek.com/biden-says-fracking-jobs-wont-chopping-block-if-hes-elected-1516793

### Did Joe Biden make any statements during the 2019 or 2020 Democratic primary debates that suggested he supported a complete ban on hydraulic fracturing (fracking)?
Answer: Yes, during the Democratic primary debates, Joe Biden made remarks that suggested he supported a ban on fracking. In a July 2019 debate, when asked if there would be any place for coal and fracking in his administration, Biden responded, "No, we would – we would work it out. We would make sure it’s eliminated and no more subsidies for either one of those..." Additionally, in a March 2020 debate, he stated he was against "new fracking." His campaign subsequently clarified both statements, explaining that he did not support a complete ban on fracking, but rather proposed "banning new oil and gas permitting on public lands and waters."

Source URL: https://www.cnn.com/2020/10/23/politics/biden-fracking-fact-check/index.html

### Did Joe Biden clarify or correct his verbal statements regarding a hydraulic fracturing (fracking) ban during the 2020 presidential debates against Donald Trump?
Answer: During the 2020 presidential debate against Donald Trump, Joe Biden did not clarify or correct his previous verbal statements, but instead "claimed that he 'never said I oppose fracking.'" It was actually his campaign that had previously clarified his confusing remarks from the Democratic primary debates, explaining that Biden's official plan proposed "banning new oil and gas permitting on public lands and waters" rather than a complete ban on fracking.

Source URL: https://www.cnn.com/2020/10/23/politics/biden-fracking-fact-check/index.html

### Did the Biden-Harris campaign issue any official statements clarifying Joe Biden's stance on hydraulic fracturing (fracking) in response to accusations of wanting a total ban in 2020?
Answer: Yes, Joe Biden directly clarified his stance during a campaign speech in Pittsburgh, stating, "I am not banning fracking. Let me say that again: I am not banning fracking. No matter how many times Donald Trump lies about me." Additionally, his campaign's climate plan did not propose a total ban on fracking, though Biden has previously expressed support for stopping "oil drilling or gas drilling on federal lands."

Source URL: https://www.uagetinvolved.org/content/joe-biden-i-am-not-banning-fracking

### Does the President of the United States have the unilateral executive authority to ban hydraulic fracturing (fracking) on private land in the United States?
Answer: Based on the provided search result, the President of the United States does not have the unilateral executive authority to ban fracking on private land. Both Republican Senator Pat Toomey and Myron Aronwitt, the Pennsylvania director of Clean Water Action, agree that the president lacks this power, with Aronwitt noting that "to completely ban fracking, you would need Congress to take action" and that a president's authority would at most only apply to federal lands.

Source URL: https://www.cbsnews.com/pittsburgh/news/toomey-introduces-bill-to-prevent-presidents-from-banning-fracking/

### Did Joe Biden's vice-presidential running mate, Kamala Harris, make any statements during the 2020 vice-presidential debate regarding the campaign's stance on banning hydraulic fracturing (fracking)?
Answer: Yes, during the 2020 vice-presidential debate, Kamala Harris stated, "Joe Biden will not end fracking," in response to claims that the campaign would ban the practice.

Source URL: https://www.politifact.com/factchecks/2020/oct/12/blog-posting/no-video-harris-2019-doesnt-prove-she-lied-about-f/

### How did major non-partisan fact-checking organizations assess Joe Biden's statements on hydraulic fracturing (fracking) during the 2020 United States presidential campaign?
Answer: During the 2020 presidential campaign, non-partisan fact-checking organization PolitiFact assessed Joe Biden's statement that he was "not banning fracking" as "Mostly True." They clarified that while Biden's position became temporarily "muddled" when he misspoke during a March primary debate, his actual policy does not propose a total ban; rather, "he wants to block the federal government from issuing new permits for drilling on public lands but would allow existing fracking operations to continue" on private land, where 90% of fracking occurs.

Source URL: https://www.inquirer.com/politics/joe-biden-pa-fracking-fact-check-20200901.html

## Final Judgement
### Step 1: Summary of Key Insights
The retrieved evidence shows that during the 2020 presidential campaign, Joe Biden's official platform did not support a complete ban on fracking; instead, he proposed banning new oil and gas leases on federal lands while allowing existing operations on private lands to continue. Biden and his running mate Kamala Harris repeatedly and explicitly stated, "I am not banning fracking." However, during the Democratic primary debates, Biden did make verbal statements suggesting he supported a ban, such as stating in July 2019 that he would make sure fracking is "eliminated" and in March 2020 that he was against "new fracking." His campaign subsequently clarified that these remarks referred only to public lands. Since the retrieved evidence clearly documents both Biden's debate remarks and his official campaign stance, no further investigation is required.

### Step 3: Conclusion
The claim that "Joe Biden said he wants to ban fracking in the US" is technically true because Biden did state during a July 2019 Democratic primary debate that he would make sure fracking is "eliminated." However, this claim is highly misleading because it excludes critical context. It ignores his official campaign platform, his campaign's subsequent clarifications, and his repeated, explicit statements during the general election asserting that he would not ban fracking. By focusing solely on his debate remarks while omitting his actual policy and clarifications, the claim creates a significantly different and inaccurate impression of his stance. Therefore, the final decision is `cherry-picking`.

### Verdict: CHERRY_PICKING

### Justification
The claim is a clear instance of cherry-picking because it relies on a verbal statement from a July 2019 Democratic primary debate where Joe Biden suggested he would make sure fracking is "eliminated" ([CNN](https://www.cnn.com/2020/10/23/politics/biden-fracking-fact-check/index.html)), while ignoring his official campaign platform and subsequent clarifications. In reality, Biden's official policy only proposed blocking new fracking leases on federal lands ([Newsweek](https://www.newsweek.com/biden-says-fracking-jobs-wont-chopping-block-if-hes-elected-1516793)), leaving fracking on private lands permitted. Both Biden and his running mate, Kamala Harris, repeatedly and explicitly stated during the general election campaign that they would not ban fracking ([Grist](https://grist.org/politics/why-wont-biden-ban-fracking-speech-pennsylvania/), [Politifact](https://www.politifact.com/factchecks/2020/oct/12/blog-posting/no-video-harris-2019-doesnt-prove-she-lied-about-f/)). By focusing solely on his debate remarks while omitting his actual policy and clarifications, the claim creates a highly misleading impression of his stance.